Forms are where most business apps earn or lose user trust. Angular's reactive forms are fully typed, which means a typo in a control name or a wrong value type is a compile error rather than a runtime surprise.

## A typed form

```typescript
import { NonNullableFormBuilder, Validators } from '@angular/forms';

export class ShippingFormComponent {
  private fb = inject(NonNullableFormBuilder);

  form = this.fb.group({
    name: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.maxLength(80)]],
    email: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.email]],
    country: ['US', Validators.required],
    express: [false],
  });

  submit() {
    if (this.form.invalid) {
      this.form.markAllAsTouched();
      return;
    }
    const value = this.form.getRawValue();
    // value is typed: { name: string; email: string; country: string; express: boolean }
  }
}
```

`NonNullableFormBuilder` means `reset()` returns controls to their initial values instead of `null`, so the types stay `string` rather than `string | null`.

## Custom validators

A validator is a function that returns `null` when valid, or an error object:

```typescript
export function noWhitespace(control: AbstractControl<string>): ValidationErrors | null {
  return control.value.trim().length === 0 && control.value.length > 0
    ? { whitespace: true }
    : null;
}
```

### Cross-field validation

Rules that involve two fields belong on the group:

```typescript
export const datesInOrder: ValidatorFn = group => {
  const start = group.get('start')?.value;
  const end = group.get('end')?.value;
  return start && end && start > end ? { datesOutOfOrder: true } : null;
};

range = this.fb.group(
  { start: [''], end: [''] },
  { validators: datesInOrder }
);
```

### Async validators

```typescript
username: ['', {
  validators: [Validators.required],
  asyncValidators: [this.usernameAvailable()],
  updateOn: 'blur',
}],
```

`updateOn: 'blur'` matters here: without it, the async check fires on every keystroke.

## Error messages people can act on

Map error keys to messages in one place:

```typescript
const MESSAGES: Record<string, (e: any) => string> = {
  required: () => 'This field is required.',
  email: () => 'Enter an email address like name@company.com.',
  maxlength: e => `Use ${e.requiredLength} characters or fewer.`,
  whitespace: () => 'This field can’t be only spaces.',
};

export function firstError(control: AbstractControl): string | null {
  const errors = control.errors;
  if (!errors) return null;
  const key = Object.keys(errors)[0];
  return MESSAGES[key]?.(errors[key]) ?? 'Check this field.';
}
```

```html
<label for="email">Email</label>
<input id="email" formControlName="email"
       [attr.aria-invalid]="form.controls.email.invalid && form.controls.email.touched"
       aria-describedby="email-error" />
@if (form.controls.email.touched && firstError(form.controls.email); as msg) {
  <p id="email-error" class="error">{{ msg }}</p>
}
```

Show errors after the user leaves a field (`touched`), not while they're still typing. And link each message to its input with `aria-describedby` so screen readers announce it.

## Dynamic fields with FormArray

```typescript
form = this.fb.group({
  contacts: this.fb.array([this.contact()]),
});

contact() {
  return this.fb.group({ name: ['', Validators.required], phone: [''] });
}

get contacts() { return this.form.controls.contacts; }

addContact() { this.contacts.push(this.contact()); }
removeContact(i: number) { this.contacts.removeAt(i); }
```

```html
<div formArrayName="contacts">
  @for (c of contacts.controls; track c; let i = $index) {
    <fieldset [formGroupName]="i">
      <legend>Contact {{ i + 1 }}</legend>
      <label [for]="'name-' + i">Name</label>
      <input [id]="'name-' + i" formControlName="name" />
      <button type="button" (click)="removeContact(i)">Remove contact {{ i + 1 }}</button>
    </fieldset>
  }
</div>
<button type="button" (click)="addContact()">Add contact</button>
```

Tracking by the control instance (`track c`) is correct here: each `FormGroup` keeps its identity for its whole life, so removing the second contact won't scramble the inputs of the third.

## Submit states

- Disable the submit button only while a request is in flight. Disabling it while the form is invalid hides *why* the user can't proceed.
- On a failed submit, call `markAllAsTouched()` and move focus to the first invalid field.
- Keep the user's input after a server error, and show what went wrong next to the button.

## Summary

Typed controls catch mistakes early, validators belong on the narrowest scope they need, error messages should say how to fix the problem, and dynamic fields are just `FormArray`s of groups. Get these right once in a shared form toolkit, and every new form in the app inherits them.

Components get most of the attention in Angular, but directives and pipes are often the better tool. If you find yourself copying the same event handler or formatting logic across templates, one of them probably belongs in your shared library.

## Attribute directives add behavior

A directive attaches behavior to an existing element without wrapping it in a new component. Here's one that auto-focuses an input when it appears:

```typescript
@Directive({ selector: '[appAutofocus]' })
export class AutofocusDirective {
  private el = inject<ElementRef<HTMLElement>>(ElementRef);

  constructor() {
    afterNextRender(() => this.el.nativeElement.focus());
  }
}
```

```html
<input appAutofocus formControlName="search" />
```

### Click outside

A common need for dropdowns and popovers:

```typescript
@Directive({
  selector: '[appClickOutside]',
  host: { '(document:click)': 'onDocumentClick($event)' },
})
export class ClickOutsideDirective {
  private el = inject(ElementRef);
  clickOutside = output<void>();

  onDocumentClick(event: MouseEvent) {
    if (!this.el.nativeElement.contains(event.target)) {
      this.clickOutside.emit();
    }
  }
}
```

```html
<div class="menu" (appClickOutside)="close()">...</div>
```

The `host` property is the modern alternative to `@HostListener` and `@HostBinding`: all host bindings live in one place in the decorator.

### Permission-based rendering

A structural directive that shows content only to users with a given permission:

```typescript
@Directive({ selector: '[appIfCan]' })
export class IfCanDirective {
  private auth = inject(AuthService);
  private tpl = inject(TemplateRef);
  private vcr = inject(ViewContainerRef);

  appIfCan = input.required<Permission>();

  constructor() {
    effect(() => {
      this.vcr.clear();
      if (this.auth.can(this.appIfCan())) {
        this.vcr.createEmbeddedView(this.tpl);
      }
    });
  }
}
```

```html
<button *appIfCan="'orders.delete'" (click)="delete()">Delete</button>
```

Hiding a button is a UX courtesy, not security. The server must still enforce the permission.

## Pipes transform values

Pipes are for display formatting. Pure pipes (the default) run only when their input changes, so they're cheap even in large lists.

```typescript
@Pipe({ name: 'relativeTime' })
export class RelativeTimePipe implements PipeTransform {
  private rtf = new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat('en', { numeric: 'auto' });

  transform(value: Date | string): string {
    const diffSec = (new Date(value).getTime() - Date.now()) / 1000;
    const units: [Intl.RelativeTimeFormatUnit, number][] = [
      ['year', 31536000], ['month', 2592000], ['day', 86400],
      ['hour', 3600], ['minute', 60], ['second', 1],
    ];
    for (const [unit, sec] of units) {
      if (Math.abs(diffSec) >= sec || unit === 'second') {
        return this.rtf.format(Math.round(diffSec / sec), unit);
      }
    }
    return '';
  }
}
```

```html
<time [attr.datetime]="order.createdAt">{{ order.createdAt | relativeTime }}</time>
```

Leaning on `Intl` APIs gives you correct localization for free.

Keep pipes pure and free of side effects. If a "pipe" needs to fetch data, it should be a service or a computed signal instead.

## Composing behavior with host directives

Host directives let a component include directive behavior without the consumer adding attributes:

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-menu-trigger',
  hostDirectives: [
    { directive: ClickOutsideDirective, outputs: ['clickOutside'] },
  ],
  template: `<ng-content />`,
})
export class MenuTriggerComponent {}
```

This is composition over inheritance: small, single-purpose directives combined into richer components.

## When to reach for which

- **Component:** it has its own template.
- **Attribute directive:** it adds behavior or styling to an element someone else renders.
- **Structural directive:** it decides whether or how often a template is rendered.
- **Pipe:** it turns a value into display text.

A shared library with a dozen well-tested directives and pipes removes a surprising amount of duplicated code from feature components.

A test that asserts `component.isOpen === true` breaks the moment you rename that field, even if the app still works. A test that clicks "Filters" and checks that the filter panel is visible keeps passing through any refactor that doesn't change behavior. Aim for the second kind.

The examples below use Jasmine-style syntax, which Jest also supports.

## Set up with TestBed

```typescript
describe('OrderListComponent', () => {
  let fixture: ComponentFixture<OrderListComponent>;
  let httpMock: HttpTestingController;

  beforeEach(async () => {
    await TestBed.configureTestingModule({
      imports: [OrderListComponent],
      providers: [provideHttpClient(), provideHttpClientTesting()],
    }).compileComponents();

    fixture = TestBed.createComponent(OrderListComponent);
    httpMock = TestBed.inject(HttpTestingController);
  });

  afterEach(() => httpMock.verify());
});
```

Standalone components go straight into `imports`. `httpMock.verify()` fails the test if any request was made that you didn't expect.

## Test what the user sees

```typescript
it('shows orders returned by the API', async () => {
  fixture.detectChanges();

  httpMock.expectOne('/api/orders').flush([
    { id: '1', customer: 'Acme', status: 'open' },
    { id: '2', customer: 'Globex', status: 'shipped' },
  ]);
  await fixture.whenStable();
  fixture.detectChanges();

  const rows = fixture.nativeElement.querySelectorAll('[data-testid="order-row"]');
  expect(rows.length).toBe(2);
  expect(rows[0].textContent).toContain('Acme');
});

it('shows an error message when loading fails', async () => {
  fixture.detectChanges();

  httpMock.expectOne('/api/orders').flush('fail', { status: 500, statusText: 'Server Error' });
  await fixture.whenStable();
  fixture.detectChanges();

  expect(fixture.nativeElement.querySelector('[role="alert"]').textContent)
    .toContain('Could not load orders');
});
```

Testing the error state matters as much as the happy path. It's the state users hit at the worst moment, and the one least likely to be checked by hand.

## Use component harnesses for library components

Angular Material and the CDK ship *harnesses*: test APIs that interact with components the way a user would, without depending on their internal DOM.

```typescript
it('filters by status', async () => {
  const loader = TestbedHarnessEnvironment.loader(fixture);
  const select = await loader.getHarness(MatSelectHarness.with({ selector: '#status' }));

  await select.open();
  await select.clickOptions({ text: 'Shipped' });

  const table = await loader.getHarness(MatTableHarness);
  const rows = await table.getRows();
  expect(rows.length).toBe(1);
});
```

When Material changes its markup in an upgrade, the harness is updated with it and your tests keep passing.

You can write harnesses for your own shared components too, by extending `ComponentHarness`. It's worth it for anything used in dozens of tests.

## Testing signal inputs

```typescript
fixture.componentRef.setInput('order', { id: '1', customer: 'Acme', status: 'open' });
fixture.detectChanges();
```

`setInput` works for both signal inputs and classic `@Input()`, and it triggers change detection correctly for `OnPush` components.

## Test services and pure logic without TestBed

Not everything needs a component fixture. A `computed`-heavy store or a pipe can be tested directly:

```typescript
it('totals the cart', () => {
  TestBed.configureTestingModule({});
  const store = TestBed.inject(CartStore);
  store.add({ id: 'a', price: 10, qty: 2 });
  store.add({ id: 'b', price: 5, qty: 1 });
  expect(store.total()).toBe(25);
});
```

Fast unit tests for logic, a smaller number of rendered component tests for behavior, and a handful of end-to-end tests (Playwright or Cypress) for the critical flows give you a healthy pyramid.

## What not to test

- Private methods and fields
- That Angular itself works (a binding updating the DOM)
- Exact CSS classes, unless the class *is* the behavior

## The payoff

When tests describe behavior, refactoring becomes safe: you change the implementation and the tests tell you whether users would notice. That confidence is what lets a team keep a large Angular codebase moving.

D3 is the most flexible charting library on the web, and Angular is a framework built around owning the DOM. Put them together naively and they fight: D3's `selection.append()` creates elements Angular doesn't know about, change detection doesn't see them, and cleanup becomes your problem.

There are two workable approaches.

## Approach 1: D3 for math, Angular for rendering

D3 is really several libraries. Its scales, shapes, and layout modules (`d3-scale`, `d3-shape`, `d3-array`) are pure functions that compute numbers and SVG path strings. They don't touch the DOM at all. Use those, and let Angular's template render the SVG.

```typescript
import { Component, computed, input } from '@angular/core';
import { scaleLinear, scaleBand } from 'd3-scale';
import { max } from 'd3-array';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-bar-chart',
  template: `
    <svg [attr.viewBox]="'0 0 ' + width + ' ' + height" role="img" [attr.aria-label]="title()">
      @for (bar of bars(); track bar.label) {
        <rect [attr.x]="bar.x" [attr.y]="bar.y" [attr.width]="bar.w" [attr.height]="bar.h">
          <title>{{ bar.label }}: {{ bar.value }}</title>
        </rect>
      }
    </svg>
  `,
})
export class BarChartComponent {
  data = input.required<{ label: string; value: number }[]>();
  title = input('Bar chart');

  width = 600;
  height = 300;

  bars = computed(() => {
    const d = this.data();
    const x = scaleBand().domain(d.map(i => i.label)).range([0, this.width]).padding(0.2);
    const y = scaleLinear().domain([0, max(d, i => i.value) ?? 0]).range([this.height, 0]);
    return d.map(i => ({
      label: i.label,
      value: i.value,
      x: x(i.label)!,
      y: y(i.value),
      w: x.bandwidth(),
      h: this.height - y(i.value),
    }));
  });
}
```

What you get:

- **Reactivity for free:** when `data` changes, `bars` recomputes and Angular updates only the changed `<rect>` elements.
- **Testability:** `bars` is a plain computed value you can assert against without rendering.
- **Accessibility:** you control every element in the template, so adding `role`, `aria-label`, and `<title>` is trivial.
- **Smaller bundles:** you import only the D3 modules you use.

This approach suits bar, line, area, and scatter charts, which covers most dashboards.

## Approach 2: hand D3 a container

Some D3 features are inherently imperative: zoom and pan behaviors, brushing, force simulations, animated transitions. For those, give D3 an element it owns completely and keep Angular out of it.

```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-force-graph',
  template: `<svg #host class="graph"></svg>`,
})
export class ForceGraphComponent {
  nodes = input.required<GraphNode[]>();
  private host = viewChild.required<ElementRef<SVGSVGElement>>('host');
  private destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);

  constructor() {
    afterNextRender(() => {
      const svg = select(this.host().nativeElement);
      const sim = forceSimulation(this.nodes()).on('tick', () => this.draw(svg));
      this.destroyRef.onDestroy(() => sim.stop());
    });
  }

  private draw(svg: Selection<SVGSVGElement, unknown, null, undefined>) { /* ... */ }
}
```

Rules for this approach:

- Initialize in `afterNextRender`, so the element exists and you skip server rendering.
- Never put Angular bindings inside the D3-owned element.
- Stop simulations and remove listeners on destroy, or you'll leak memory each time the route changes.
- For high-frequency ticks, consider running outside Angular's zone (`NgZone.runOutsideAngular`) if your app still uses Zone.js, so each tick doesn't trigger change detection.

## Which to choose

Start with approach 1. Switch to approach 2 only when you need behavior D3 implements imperatively. In a larger dashboard you'll often mix both: static charts rendered by Angular, and one interactive graph that D3 owns outright.

"Which state management library should we use?" is often asked too early. Many Angular apps are well served by a plain service holding signals. The trick is knowing when that stops being enough.

## Level 1: a service with signals

```typescript
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class CartStore {
  private readonly _items = signal<CartItem[]>([]);

  readonly items = this._items.asReadonly();
  readonly count = computed(() => this._items().length);
  readonly total = computed(() =>
    this._items().reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0)
  );

  add(item: CartItem) {
    this._items.update(items => [...items, item]);
  }

  remove(id: string) {
    this._items.update(items => items.filter(i => i.id !== id));
  }
}
```

The important details:

- State is private and writable; the public API is read-only (`asReadonly()`). Components can't change state except through named methods.
- Updates are immutable (`[...items, item]`), so `computed` values and `OnPush` components see the change.
- Derived values are `computed`, never duplicated state.

This scales surprisingly far: one store per feature, scoped with route-level `providers` when it shouldn't outlive the feature.

## Level 2: NgRx Signal Store

When several features follow the same pattern (load a collection, track loading and error, select an item), the boilerplate repeats. `@ngrx/signals` gives you a structured, composable version of the service above:

```typescript
export const OrdersStore = signalStore(
  { providedIn: 'root' },
  withState({ orders: [] as Order[], loading: false, error: null as string | null }),
  withComputed(({ orders }) => ({
    openOrders: computed(() => orders().filter(o => o.status === 'open')),
  })),
  withMethods((store, api = inject(OrdersApi)) => ({
    async load() {
      patchState(store, { loading: true, error: null });
      try {
        patchState(store, { orders: await api.list(), loading: false });
      } catch {
        patchState(store, { loading: false, error: 'Could not load orders.' });
      }
    },
  }))
);
```

It's still signals underneath, so components read `store.openOrders()` exactly as before. You also get reusable features (like entity collections) and a consistent shape across the team.

## Level 3: NgRx Store (Redux pattern)

The classic store, with actions, reducers, effects, and selectors, costs more ceremony. In return you get:

- **A log of everything that happened.** Redux DevTools shows each action and lets you replay state, which is invaluable when debugging complex flows.
- **Decoupling.** A component dispatches `orderSubmitted`; any number of effects and reducers can react without the component knowing about them.
- **Enforced conventions** across a large team.

## Signs you've outgrown a simple service

- Several features update the same state, and you can't tell who changed what.
- You need undo or redo, optimistic updates with rollback, or time-travel debugging.
- Side effects chain together ("when an order is submitted, refresh inventory, then notify the user").
- New team members keep inventing different patterns for the same problem.

## My default

Start with signal services. Move a feature to Signal Store when the boilerplate starts repeating. Reach for the full NgRx Store when the app's complexity is really about *events* and how they interact, not just about holding data.

Whichever you choose, the rules don't change: keep state private, expose it read-only, update immutably, and derive instead of duplicating.

Accessibility often gets treated as an audit at the end of a project. It's far cheaper as a habit while building components. Most issues come down to four things: semantic HTML, keyboard support, focus management, and announcing changes. Angular and its Component Dev Kit (CDK) help with all four.

## 1. Use the native element first

```html
<!-- Inaccessible -->
<div class="btn" (click)="save()">Save</div>

<!-- Accessible, with no extra work -->
<button type="button" (click)="save()">Save</button>
```

The `<div>` isn't focusable, doesn't respond to Enter or Space, and isn't announced as a button. Fixing that by hand takes `tabindex`, `role`, and a keydown handler, and it's easy to get one wrong. The `<button>` does all of it for free.

The same applies to links (`<a href>` for navigation), form labels (`<label for>`), and headings (in order, no skipped levels).

## 2. Every input needs a name

```html
<label for="email">Work email</label>
<input id="email" type="email" formControlName="email"
       [attr.aria-invalid]="email.invalid && email.touched"
       aria-describedby="email-error" />

@if (email.invalid && email.touched) {
  <p id="email-error">Enter an email address like name&#64;company.com.</p>
}
```

Placeholder text is not a label: it disappears when the user types, and its contrast is usually too low. Error messages should say what's wrong and how to fix it, and be linked to the field with `aria-describedby`.

## 3. Manage focus when the UI changes

When a dialog opens, focus should move into it, stay trapped inside, and return to the trigger when it closes. The CDK handles this:

```typescript
import { Dialog } from '@angular/cdk/dialog';

export class OrdersComponent {
  private dialog = inject(Dialog);

  openFilters() {
    this.dialog.open(FilterDialogComponent, { ariaLabelledBy: 'filter-title' });
  }
}
```

For your own overlays, `cdkTrapFocus` keeps Tab inside a region:

```html
<div class="panel" cdkTrapFocus [cdkTrapFocusAutoCapture]="true">...</div>
```

After route changes, move focus to the new page's main heading so screen reader users know the page changed:

```typescript
this.router.events.pipe(filter(e => e instanceof NavigationEnd))
  .subscribe(() => document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('main h1')?.focus());
```

(Give that `h1` `tabindex="-1"` so it can receive focus.)

## 4. Announce changes that happen without a page load

When a list filters or a save succeeds, sighted users see it; screen reader users hear nothing unless you tell them. Use `LiveAnnouncer`:

```typescript
import { LiveAnnouncer } from '@angular/cdk/a11y';

export class SearchComponent {
  private announcer = inject(LiveAnnouncer);

  onResults(count: number) {
    this.announcer.announce(`${count} results`, 'polite');
  }
}
```

## 5. Keyboard navigation in composite widgets

Menus, listboxes, and tab lists should move with arrow keys, not Tab. `ActiveDescendantKeyManager` and `FocusKeyManager` from `@angular/cdk/a11y` implement the WAI-ARIA patterns so you don't have to:

```typescript
@ViewChildren(OptionComponent) options!: QueryList<OptionComponent>;
private keyManager!: FocusKeyManager<OptionComponent>;

ngAfterViewInit() {
  this.keyManager = new FocusKeyManager(this.options).withWrap().withTypeAhead();
}

onKeydown(event: KeyboardEvent) {
  this.keyManager.onKeydown(event);
}
```

## 6. Visual checks

- **Contrast:** body text at least 4.5:1, large text 3:1 (WCAG 1.4.3).
- **Focus visible:** never remove outlines without a replacement. `:focus-visible` lets you show rings for keyboard users only.
- **Don't rely on color alone:** pair a red error border with text or an icon.
- **Reduced motion:** respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for animations.

## 7. Test it

- Unplug the mouse and complete the main task with the keyboard.
- Run axe (browser extension or `axe-core` in your test suite) on each page.
- Try VoiceOver (macOS) or NVDA (Windows) on one key flow per release.

Automated tools catch maybe a third of issues. The keyboard test catches a surprising share of the rest.

## The habit

Treat accessibility like types: part of the component's contract, checked as you build. A component that's accessible from the start never needs a retrofit.

Most RxJS bugs in Angular apps come from picking the wrong flattening operator. All four take an outer stream (clicks, keystrokes, route changes) and map each value to an inner Observable (usually an HTTP call). They differ in what happens when a new value arrives while a request is still running.

## The four operators

| Operator | When a new value arrives mid-request | Use it for |
|---|---|---|
| `switchMap` | Cancels the old request | Search, route params, filters |
| `concatMap` | Queues the new one until the old finishes | Ordered saves, sequential updates |
| `exhaustMap` | Ignores the new one | Submit buttons, login |
| `mergeMap` | Runs both in parallel | Independent uploads, bulk fetches |

### switchMap: only the latest matters

```typescript
product$ = this.route.paramMap.pipe(
  map(params => params.get('id')!),
  switchMap(id => this.api.getProduct(id))
);
```

If the user clicks through three products quickly, only the last response is shown. With `mergeMap`, a slow first response could arrive last and overwrite the correct product. That's a race condition you'd struggle to reproduce.

### exhaustMap: no double submits

```typescript
this.save$.pipe(
  exhaustMap(() => this.api.saveOrder(this.form.value))
).subscribe();
```

While the save is in flight, extra clicks are dropped. You should still disable the button, but this protects you even if the UI doesn't.

### concatMap: order matters

```typescript
this.edits$.pipe(
  concatMap(edit => this.api.patch(edit))
).subscribe();
```

Each PATCH waits for the previous one, so the server sees edits in the order the user made them.

## Handle errors inside the inner Observable

This is the mistake I see most:

```typescript
// Wrong: one failed request kills the whole stream
search$ = this.query$.pipe(
  switchMap(q => this.api.search(q)),
  catchError(() => of([]))
);
```

When `catchError` sits on the outer stream, the first error completes it. Every later search silently does nothing. Put it on the inner one:

```typescript
search$ = this.query$.pipe(
  switchMap(q => this.api.search(q).pipe(
    catchError(() => of([]))
  ))
);
```

## Model loading and error as data

Instead of three separate flags, emit one state object:

```typescript
type Load<T> =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'ok'; data: T }
  | { status: 'error'; message: string };

product$ = this.id$.pipe(
  switchMap(id => this.api.getProduct(id).pipe(
    map(data => ({ status: 'ok', data }) as const),
    catchError(() => of({ status: 'error', message: 'Could not load this product.' } as const)),
    startWith({ status: 'loading' } as const)
  ))
);
```

```html
@let state = productState();
@switch (state.status) {
  @case ('loading') { <app-skeleton /> }
  @case ('error') { <p role="alert">{{ state.message }}</p> }
  @case ('ok') { <app-product [product]="state.data" /> }
}
```

In the component, expose it to the template with `productState = toSignal(this.product$, { initialValue: { status: 'loading' } as const });`.

The type makes impossible states impossible: you can't be "loading" and "error" at the same time.

## Retrying with backoff

```typescript
this.api.getProduct(id).pipe(
  retry({ count: 2, delay: (_, attempt) => timer(attempt * 1000) })
)
```

Retry only idempotent requests (GETs). Retrying a POST can create duplicate orders.

## Summary

Pick the operator by asking what should happen to an in-flight request when a new one arrives. Then catch errors on the inner Observable, and represent loading state as a single typed value. Those three habits remove most async bugs from an Angular codebase.

When Signals arrived in Angular, a common question was whether RxJS was on its way out. It isn't. They answer different questions, and most real apps use both.

**A signal is a value that changes.** You can read it at any time and it always has a current value.

**An Observable is a stream of events.** It may never emit, emit once, or emit forever, and it can be cancelled.

## Signals for component and UI state

```typescript
import { Component, signal, computed } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-cart',
  template: `
    <p>{{ itemCount() }} items · {{ total() | currency }}</p>
    <button (click)="clear()">Clear cart</button>
  `,
})
export class CartComponent {
  items = signal<CartItem[]>([]);

  itemCount = computed(() => this.items().length);
  total = computed(() => this.items().reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price * i.qty, 0));

  clear() {
    this.items.set([]);
  }
}
```

`computed` values are lazy and memoized: `total` recalculates only when `items` changes, and only if something reads it. There's no subscription to manage and nothing to unsubscribe from.

Use signals for:

- Form and UI state (open/closed, selected tab, filters)
- Derived values (`computed`)
- State shared through a service that components read synchronously

## RxJS for async workflows

RxJS shines when *time* is part of the problem: debouncing, cancellation, retries, combining streams.

```typescript
results$ = this.query$.pipe(
  debounceTime(300),
  distinctUntilChanged(),
  switchMap(q => this.api.search(q).pipe(
    catchError(() => of([]))
  ))
);
```

`switchMap` cancels the previous request when a new query arrives. Doing that with signals alone means hand-writing cancellation logic.

Use RxJS for:

- HTTP requests and anything cancellable
- User input that needs debouncing or throttling
- WebSockets and server-sent events
- Combining several async sources with timing rules

## Bridging the two

`@angular/core/rxjs-interop` gives you converters in both directions:

```typescript
import { toSignal, toObservable } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

export class SearchComponent {
  query = signal('');

  results = toSignal(
    toObservable(this.query).pipe(
      debounceTime(300),
      switchMap(q => this.api.search(q))
    ),
    { initialValue: [] }
  );
}
```

The component keeps a simple signal for the input, RxJS handles the timing and cancellation, and the template reads `results()` like any other signal. `toSignal` also unsubscribes automatically when the component is destroyed.

## About effect()

`effect()` runs side effects when signals change. It's tempting to use it to copy one signal into another. Don't: that's what `computed` (or `linkedSignal` for writable derived state) is for. Keep `effect()` for things that leave Angular's world: logging, `localStorage`, imperative third-party libraries.

## A rule of thumb

- If you'd describe it as "the current X," use a **signal**.
- If you'd describe it as "when X happens, do Y," use an **Observable**.
- Convert at the boundary with `toSignal` and `toObservable`, and let each tool do what it's good at.

For years, Angular templates used structural directives: `*ngIf`, `*ngFor`, `*ngSwitch`. They worked, but they needed imports, the `else` syntax was clumsy, and `trackBy` required a separate method. Built-in control flow replaces all of them with blocks that are part of the template language itself.

## @if

```html
@if (user(); as u) {
  <p>Welcome back, {{ u.name }}</p>
} @else if (loading()) {
  <app-spinner />
} @else {
  <a routerLink="/login">Sign in</a>
}
```

No `ng-template`, no template reference variables. The `as` alias narrows the type, so inside the block the compiler knows `u` isn't null.

## @for and why `track` is required

```html
@for (order of orders(); track order.id) {
  <app-order-row [order]="order" />
} @empty {
  <p>No orders yet.</p>
}
```

`track` is mandatory, and that's a good thing. It tells Angular how to match items between renders. With a stable key like `order.id`, Angular moves existing DOM nodes instead of destroying and recreating them when the list changes. That keeps focus, scroll position, and component state intact.

Common mistakes:

- **`track $index`** on a list that gets sorted or filtered. Every item now "changes" when its position does, so you lose the benefit.
- **`track order`** (the object itself) when your data layer returns new objects on every fetch. Identity changes every time, so everything re-renders.

Use a real ID whenever you have one.

`@for` also gives you contextual variables: `$index`, `$first`, `$last`, `$even`, `$odd`, `$count`.

## @switch

```html
@switch (status()) {
  @case ('shipped') { <app-badge tone="success" label="Shipped" /> }
  @case ('pending') { <app-badge tone="warning" label="Pending" /> }
  @default { <app-badge label="Unknown" /> }
}
```

## @defer: lazy loading without the router

`@defer` is the most powerful of the four. It splits the contents into a separate JavaScript chunk and loads it only when a trigger fires.

```html
@defer (on viewport) {
  <app-sales-chart [data]="sales()" />
} @placeholder (minimum 300ms) {
  <div class="chart-skeleton"></div>
} @loading (after 150ms; minimum 400ms) {
  <app-spinner />
} @error {
  <p>The chart failed to load. <button (click)="retry()">Retry</button></p>
}
```

Triggers include:

- `on viewport`: when the placeholder scrolls into view
- `on idle`: when the browser is idle (the default)
- `on interaction`: on click or keydown on the placeholder
- `on hover`, `on timer(2s)`, `on immediate`
- `when condition()`: when a signal or expression becomes truthy

A heavy chart library below the fold is the ideal use. It's no longer in the initial bundle, and users who never scroll down never download it.

The `minimum` and `after` options exist to prevent flicker: a spinner that flashes for 50ms looks broken.

## Migrating

The Angular CLI ships a schematic that rewrites existing templates:

```bash
ng generate @angular/core:control-flow
```

Run it, review the diff, and pay special attention to every `track` expression it generates. It can't always infer the right key, so check that each one is a stable ID.

## Takeaway

The new syntax is shorter and the type checking is tighter, but the real win is `@defer`: lazy loading moves from a router-level decision to a one-line template decision.
